or  34 years he ruined people's lives with  his homophobia and so-called therapy and  he is gay himself that's a tragedy for  everyone involved is a tragedy he  couldn't live his life as the person he  truly is it's a tragedy that he ruined  so many people's lives with his  so-called conversion therapy uh now he's  also a Mormon and he blames a lot of the  homophobia his homophobia on the Mormon  Church now that I believe the Mormon  Church is homophobic but blaming it just  on the church gets him out of criticism  that he still deserves he not all  Mormons are ex gay therapists that is  still something that he chose to do so  it's great that he came out but then the  biggest tragedy at the bottom of this is  that he still seems to think that this  so-called ex-gay therapy is legitimate  because he also added in his facebook  statement about this what you can take  from this is that my time in a straight  marriage and in the ex-gay world was  genuine and sincere and a rich blessing  for me to me I remember most of it with  fondness and gratitude for the joy and  growth it caused in me and many others  he's still pretending or believes that  ex-gay therapy is legitimate and good  and that it caused joy and growth for  the people that he interacted with as an  ex-gay therapist so this is I mean I  guess it's good in a sense that he can  now live as the person he is it seems as  though he's getting out of ex-gay  therapy but he still is touting the idea  that it's a good thing fortunately if we  want to find like a silver lining in  this entire thing ex-gay therapy  reparative therapy has been banned for  minors in fifteen states in fifteen  states including some of the highest  population ones like California and  Illinois and New York even if the  parents want to send the kid to ex-gay  therapy it's illegal to send minors to  it now our church is finding ways to get  around this or the religiously  affiliated ex-gay therapists figuring  out ways to get around this yes of  course they are but strictly speaking  it's still a success  the bad part is that it's unfortunately  still legal for adults everywhere and  for children in 35 states so progress  has been made  not nearly enough and you know the every  time the entire homophobic people are  actually gay thing comes up it's  important to just remind everybody that  most people who are homophobic are not  gay right and it actually doesn't even  really matter if they are homophobia is  something we can activate and argue  against regardless of whether the people  who are homophobic are doing it because  they are resenting and trying to repress  their own sexuality or because they just  think hey the Bible says I should be  homophobic so I am of course there are  some people who are using their  homophobia as a way to hide their  homosexuality of course that happens  it's not most people and I think that  sometimes the the pro LGBT rights groups  will go a little far with the memes and  the jokes about everybody who's  homophobic actually being gay I don't  think we really gain that much from it  but certainly there's no doubt that  there have been dozens of prominent  homophobes who were gay or bisexual so  much more work to be done 15 states have  at least done something good here
 

